---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Table Saws",
    "url": "/best-table-saw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Circular Saws",
    "url": "/best-cordless-circular-saw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Reciprocating Saws",
    "url": "/best-reciprocating-saw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Tool Brand",
    "url": "/best-cordless-tool-brand/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Milwaukee vs DeWalt",
    "url": "/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Jigsaws",
    "url": "/best-jigsaw/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-06", modified: "2026-10-06" });
const title = "6 Best Miter Saws in 2026: Sliders, Chop Saws and Cordless";
const description =
  "Six miter saws compared on crosscut capacity, blade size, bevel and slide design, plus when a 10-inch chop saw is enough and when a 12-inch slider pays off.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dws780",
    "name": "DeWalt 12\" Double-Bevel Sliding Compound Miter Saw with XPS (DWS780)",
    "category": "12\" Dual-Bevel Slider, 15 Amp Corded",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 2500,
    "pros": [
      "Cuts up to 2x14 lumber at 90 degrees, with roughly 16 inches of maximum crosscut width",
      "XPS LED shadow line shows the cut without any laser to calibrate",
      "Tall sliding fences hold 7-1/2\" crown nested and 6-3/4\" base vertically",
      "Miters 60 degrees right and 50 degrees left; bevels both ways",
      "15 amp, 3,800 RPM motor that has been the jobsite standard for years"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Close to 60 lbs, so it is a saw you set up once, not one you carry around",
      "Horizontal rails need a lot of clear space behind the saw"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The 12-inch slider to buy if you want one saw that handles framing lumber, wide shelving and tall trim without compromise.",
    "description": "The DWS780 has been the default answer to which miter saw a carpenter should buy for long enough that most trim crews have one, and the reasons still hold. It is a 12-inch dual-bevel slider with a 15 amp, 3,800 RPM motor, and DeWalt rates it to cut 2x14 lumber at 90 degrees with around 16 inches of total crosscut width, which covers stair treads, wide shelving and most framing. The tall sliding fences support 7-1/2 inches of crown nested against the fence and 6-3/4 inches of base standing vertically, so most residential trim never needs to be laid flat and cut as a compound angle. The feature that separates it from its cheaper sibling is XPS: an LED mounted beside the blade casts the blade's shadow onto the workpiece, so the shadow edge is the cut line. Unlike a laser, it never drifts out of calibration and works the same after a blade change. The trade-offs are size and weight. It weighs close to 60 lbs and its horizontal rails slide backward, so it needs a stand and clear space behind it. If you never cut anything wider than a 2x10, a 10-inch slider will do the same work for less money."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dws779",
    "name": "DeWalt 12\" Double-Bevel Sliding Compound Miter Saw (DWS779)",
    "category": "12\" Dual-Bevel Slider, 15 Amp Corded",
    "badge": "Best Value 12-Inch",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 9000,
    "pros": [
      "Same 15 amp, 3,800 RPM motor and rail system as the DWS780",
      "Cuts 2x14 at 90 degrees and 2x10 at 45 degrees",
      "Dual bevel and tall sliding fences for crown and base",
      "Usually costs noticeably less than the XPS model",
      "Among the most-rated miter saws on Amazon, with a 4.8 average"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No XPS shadow line, so you mark and sight cuts the old way",
      "Just as heavy and as deep as the DWS780"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The DWS780 without the cut-line light, and the smarter buy for anyone who marks a pencil line and sights the blade anyway.",
    "description": "The DWS779 is the reason many people end up not buying the DWS780. It shares the 15 amp, 3,800 RPM motor, the dual horizontal steel rails, the dual-bevel head and the tall sliding fences, and its cutting capacities are close to identical: DeWalt rates it at 2x14 at 90 degrees and 2x10 at 45 degrees. What it gives up is the XPS LED shadow line. That light is genuinely useful, especially when you are cutting to a pencil mark quickly and repeatedly, but plenty of finish carpenters lower the blade, line a tooth up with the mark and cut, which is all the XPS system really automates. If that is how you already work, the DWS779 gives you the same saw for less money, and it shows in the numbers: it carries a 4.8 average across roughly 9,000 Amazon ratings, one of the largest rating counts in the category. The downsides are the same as the DWS780's. It is a big, heavy 12-inch slider, its rails need clear space behind the saw, and you will want a proper stand. For a homeowner cutting deck boards, fence pickets and baseboard, this is the 12-inch saw we would buy."
  },
  {
    "id": "bosch-gcm12sd",
    "name": "Bosch 12\" Dual-Bevel Axial-Glide Miter Saw (GCM12SD)",
    "category": "12\" Dual-Bevel Glide, 15 Amp Corded",
    "badge": "Best for Tight Spaces",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 1900,
    "pros": [
      "Axial-Glide arm replaces rails, so the saw can sit much closer to a wall",
      "14 inches of crosscut at 90 degrees and 9-3/4 inches at 45",
      "Very smooth, repeatable slide action on sealed bearings",
      "Bevels 48 degrees both ways with controls at the front of the saw",
      "6-1/2\" crown nested against the fence"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 65 lbs, the heaviest saw on this list",
      "Usually priced above the DeWalt 12-inch saws"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The 12-inch slider for a garage or basement shop where the saw has to live against a wall.",
    "description": "Bosch's GCM12SD solves the most annoying problem with sliding miter saws: the rails. A conventional slider pushes its rails out the back of the saw, so it needs a foot or more of empty space behind it. The GCM12SD instead mounts the motor on a pair of hinged arms that fold and unfold as you pull the head toward you, much like a human elbow. The saw can sit close to a wall, which is exactly what a crowded garage or basement shop needs. It does not give up capacity to get there. Bosch rates it at 14 inches of crosscut at 90 degrees and 9-3/4 inches at 45 degrees, it miters 52 degrees left and 60 degrees right, and it bevels 48 degrees both ways with the bevel controls at the front of the saw instead of behind it. The glide action on sealed bearings is smooth enough that many owners consider it the nicest-feeling slide on any saw. The cost is weight and price. At about 65 lbs it is heavier than the DeWalt saws, and it is usually priced above them. If your saw will stay in one place against a wall, both trade-offs are easy to live with."
  },
  {
    "id": "makita-ls1019l",
    "name": "Makita 10\" Dual-Bevel Sliding Compound Miter Saw with Laser (LS1019L)",
    "category": "10\" Dual-Bevel Slider, 15 Amp Corded",
    "badge": "Best 10-Inch Slider",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 470,
    "pros": [
      "Fixed two-rail system lets the saw operate flush against a wall",
      "12 inches of crosscut at 90 degrees from a 10-inch blade",
      "Direct-drive 15 amp motor with soft start and electronic speed control",
      "Miters 60 degrees left and right; bevels 48 degrees both ways",
      "Cuts 6-5/8\" crown nested and a 4x4 in one pass"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 58 lbs, as heavy as many 12-inch saws",
      "Lasers need occasional adjustment to stay on the cut line"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A precise 10-inch slider for trim and furniture work, with a rail design that keeps it close to the wall.",
    "description": "The LS1019L is the 10-inch saw for people who want 12-inch crosscut capacity without 12-inch blades. Makita rates it at 12 inches of crosscut at 90 degrees and 8-1/2 inches at 45, and it handles 6-5/8-inch crown nested, 5-1/4-inch base standing vertically and a 4x4 in a single pass. The rails are the clever part. Instead of sliding back with the head, the two steel rails are fixed to an arm at the back of the saw and the head rides along them, so the saw can be pushed tight against a wall, much like the Bosch glide saw. The motor is direct drive, 15 amps and 3,200 RPM, with soft start and electronic speed control that holds speed under load, which helps keep cuts clean in hardwood. Bevel adjustment is up front. The laser can be set for the left or right side of the blade, though like all lasers it needs checking after rough transport. The catch is weight: at around 58 lbs it is as heavy as many 12-inch saws, so it is not the portable 10-inch saw some buyers expect. Its rating count on Amazon is modest, so we lean on its specification and long track record more than on reviews."
  },
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-2734-20",
    "name": "Milwaukee M18 FUEL 10\" Dual-Bevel Sliding Compound Miter Saw (2734-20)",
    "category": "10\" Dual-Bevel Slider, 18V Cordless",
    "badge": "Best Cordless",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 60,
    "pros": [
      "Milwaukee says up to 400 cuts per charge on an HD9.0 battery",
      "Cuts 2x12 at 90 degrees and 2x8 at a 45-degree miter",
      "About 45 lbs, light enough to carry to the work",
      "LED shadow cut line, cam miter lock and stainless detent plate",
      "4,000 RPM brushless motor on the M18 platform"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs a large, expensive M18 battery to work well",
      "Smaller vertical capacity than the 12-inch corded saws"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cordless slider for M18 owners who cut on decks, in driveways and in rooms without a handy outlet.",
    "description": "Cordless miter saws used to be compromise tools for trim carpenters doing punch lists. Milwaukee's 2734-20 is a full 10-inch dual-bevel slider that happens to run on an 18-volt battery. Milwaukee rates it at 2x12 crosscuts at 90 degrees and 2x8 at a 45-degree miter, with a 4,000 RPM brushless motor, 50 and 60 degree miter stops, 48 degrees of bevel each way, 5-3/4 inches of base against the fence and 5-1/4 inches of nested crown. The company claims up to 400 cuts per charge with its HD9.0 pack, which is a full day of trim for most people. It weighs about 45 lbs, which is still heavy, but it is the difference between carrying a saw to the back deck and setting up a station in the garage and walking every board back and forth. A shadow-line LED shows the cut, and the cam-locking miter and adjustable stainless detent plate are proper pro features. The weak points are the battery and the price: the saw is meaningfully worse with small packs, so budget for a big one if you do not already own it. If you are not on M18, DeWalt's 60V FLEXVOLT DCS781 is the 12-inch cordless alternative."
  },
  {
    "id": "metabo-hpt-c10fcgs",
    "name": "Metabo HPT 10\" Single-Bevel Compound Miter Saw (C10FCGS)",
    "category": "10\" Single-Bevel Chop Saw, 15 Amp Corded",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 9900,
    "pros": [
      "About 24 lbs, easy to carry and store on a shelf",
      "15 amp, 5,000 RPM motor cuts dimensional lumber quickly",
      "Miters 0 to 52 degrees left and right",
      "Five-year warranty from Metabo HPT",
      "Close to 10,000 Amazon ratings with a 4.7 average"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No slide: crosscut tops out around 2x6 at 90 degrees",
      "Bevels left only, so some compound cuts need the board flipped"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The miter saw to buy if you mostly cut 2x4s, 2x6s, fence pickets and baseboard, and do not want to spend slider money.",
    "description": "Most homeowners do not need a sliding saw, and the C10FCGS is the proof. It is a plain 10-inch compound chop saw with a 15 amp, 5,000 RPM motor, a 0-to-52-degree miter range in both directions and a 0-to-45-degree bevel to the left. Without rails, its crosscut capacity is limited to roughly 5-5/8 inches at 90 degrees, which Metabo HPT translates as a 2x6 at 90 degrees or a 2x4 at 45. That covers framing a shed wall, cutting deck joists to length, trimming fence pickets and mitering standard baseboard, which is most of what a homeowner's miter saw ever does. At around 24 lbs it is light enough to carry with one hand and store on a shelf, and it comes with a five-year warranty. It is also enormously popular, with close to 10,000 Amazon ratings averaging 4.7. The limitations are real. Anything wider than a 2x6 needs two passes and a flip, which rarely lines up perfectly, and the single bevel means some compound cuts require flipping the workpiece. Metabo HPT has since released a next-generation C10FCG2 with a shadow-line cut indicator; either is a good buy."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Miter Saw",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-miter-saw/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I buy a 10-inch or 12-inch miter saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buy a 12-inch slider if you build decks, cut stair treads, handle posts or install tall baseboard and crown. Buy a 10-inch saw for trim, furniture and general homeowner work. The 10-inch blades are cheaper, deflect less and the saws are lighter. A 10-inch slider reaches 12 inches of crosscut, which is enough for most shelving and trim."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a sliding miter saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if you regularly cut boards wider than about 6 inches. A 10-inch chop saw handles a 2x6 at 90 degrees, which covers framing, fence pickets, deck joists and most baseboard. A slider cuts 2x12 or 2x14 in one pass. If wide boards are rare, finish them with a circular saw and save the money and weight."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a dual-bevel miter saw worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is worth it for trim work. A dual-bevel saw tilts both ways, so you can bevel both sides of a corner without flipping long boards end for end. For framing, decks and fences, a single-bevel saw is fine because you rarely cut bevels. For whole-house baseboard and crown, dual bevel saves real time and mistakes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cordless miter saws as good as corded?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The best ones are close. The Milwaukee 2734-20 is a full 10-inch dual-bevel slider rated for up to 400 cuts per charge on a large pack. Corded saws still cost less and never run down. Buy cordless if you already own big batteries and work in places without convenient outlets; otherwise a corded saw is the better value."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a miter saw replace a table saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. A miter saw crosscuts and miters, cutting across the width of a board. It cannot rip a board along its length or cut down sheets of plywood. Most workshops end up with both, or with a miter saw and a circular saw with a straightedge for ripping. Decide which cuts you make most often before buying either."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What blade should I put on my miter saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For framing, the included general-purpose blade is fine. For trim and furniture, fit a finish blade with 60 to 80 teeth on a 10-inch saw or 80 to 100 teeth on a 12-inch saw. Use blades designed for miter saws, with a low or negative hook angle, and a non-ferrous blade for aluminum."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I check if my miter saw is square?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Cut a wide board at 90 degrees, flip one half over and push the cut edges together. Any gap shows double the error. For 45 degrees, cut two pieces of trim and dry-fit the corner against a square. Adjust the detent plate or bevel stop per the manual. Recheck after transporting the saw, since bumps shift settings."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best miter saw",
      "sliding compound miter saw",
      "10 inch vs 12 inch miter saw",
      "dual bevel miter saw",
      "cordless miter saw",
      "miter saw for beginners"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            6 Saws Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Miter Saws</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A miter saw is the fastest way to make square, repeatable crosscuts and clean miters. The right one depends on the widest board you cut every month, not the biggest number on the box.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#dewalt-dws780"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#sizes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. 10-Inch vs 12-Inch</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Types Decoded</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#capacity" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Capacity Compared</a>
          <a href="#cordless" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Cordless vs Corded</a>
          <a href="#accuracy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Accuracy and Setup</a>
          <a href="#blades" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Blades</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Safety Habits</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people buy more miter saw than they need, and then find it heavier and harder to store than they expected. A 12-inch slider is the right tool for decks, stairs and big trim. A 10-inch chop saw is the right tool for almost everything else a homeowner cuts. This guide covers both, plus the cordless saws that are finally good enough to be the only saw you own.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people building decks, fences, sheds and trim, the best miter saw is a 12-inch dual-bevel slider like the DeWalt DWS779 or DWS780. It cuts boards up to 2x14, handles tall baseboard and crown standing up against the fence, and it is the saw you will not outgrow. If your saw has to live against a wall, the Bosch GCM12SD's glide arm does the same work in far less depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only ever cut 2x4s, 2x6s, fence pickets and standard baseboard, you do not need a slider at all. A 10-inch chop saw like the Metabo HPT C10FCGS weighs about 24 lbs, costs a fraction of a 12-inch slider and makes the cuts most homeowners actually make. Plenty of people buy a big slider, then use it for nothing a chop saw could not do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cordless miter saws are now good enough to be a first choice rather than a backup, but only if you already own the batteries. The Milwaukee 2734-20 is a real 10-inch slider that runs a full day on a large M18 pack. Buying a cordless miter saw plus a big battery from scratch usually costs more than a corded saw that does the same job.
          </p>

          <p>
            A miter saw does one thing, crosscutting and mitering, and it does that faster and more accurately than anything else. It does not rip boards along their length. If you need to rip sheet goods or narrow boards, that is a job for a <a href="/best-table-saw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">table saw</a> or a <a href="/best-cordless-circular-saw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">circular saw</a> with a straightedge, and most shops end up with a miter saw and one of those.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 10-Inch vs 12-Inch: What the Blade Size Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blade diameter sets two things: how thick a board the saw can cut through, and how wide a board it can cut across in combination with the slide. A 12-inch blade cuts deeper, which is why 12-inch saws handle 4x4 and 4x6 posts and tall baseboard standing vertically with less fuss. A 10-inch blade spins faster at the rim, deflects less, and costs noticeably less to replace with a high-quality finish blade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Width is mostly about the slide, not the blade. A 10-inch chop saw tops out around 5-1/2 inches, enough for a 2x6. Add rails and the same 10-inch blade reaches 12 inches, as the Makita LS1019L and Milwaukee 2734-20 do. A 12-inch slider reaches 14 inches or more. A 12-inch chop saw without a slide lands around 2x8, which is why we no longer think it is the best middle ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest case for 10 inches is precision and cost. Finish blades for 10-inch saws are cheaper and more widely stocked, the smaller blade wanders less in hardwood, and a 10-inch saw is lighter to move. The honest case for 12 inches is capacity. If you build decks, cut stair treads or handle 5-1/4-inch and taller baseboard, a 12-inch slider means you stop flipping boards to finish a cut.
          </p>

