        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Chop, Sliding, Compound, Dual-Bevel: The Types Decoded
          </h2>

          <p>
            A miter saw pivots its table left and right to cut miters, the angled cuts at picture frame and baseboard corners. A compound miter saw also tilts its head to cut bevels, and doing both at once gives you a compound cut, which is how crown molding is cut when it lies flat. Every saw on this list is a compound saw. The useful distinctions are whether it slides and which way it bevels.
          </p>

          <p>
            A single-bevel saw tilts in one direction, usually left. You can still cut a matching bevel the other way by flipping the board end for end, and for occasional work that is fine. A dual-bevel saw tilts both ways, so you cut both sides of a corner without flipping long or awkward trim. For anyone running baseboard and crown through a whole house, dual bevel saves real time and mistakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            A sliding saw adds rails or arms so the head can travel toward you through the cut, which is how a 10-inch blade crosscuts a 12-inch board. The traditional design uses horizontal rails that slide out the back of the saw. Bosch's axial glide and Makita's fixed-rail system both keep the saw's footprint shallow, which matters in a garage where the saw sits against the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the cut-line indicator. Lasers project a line next to the blade and need calibrating. Shadow-line LEDs, like DeWalt's XPS and Milwaukee's version, shine past the blade so its shadow falls exactly where it will cut, and stay accurate after a blade change. We prefer shadow lines, but neither replaces a sharp pencil mark and the habit of lowering the blade to check.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy for the widest board you cut every month, not the widest board you might cut once.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 2x12 stair tread you cut once a decade can be finished with a circular saw. The 2x6 deck boards you cut every weekend should go through in one pass.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

