        <div id="cordless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cordless vs Corded Miter Saws
          </h2>

          <p>
            A corded 15 amp saw never runs out, never slows down on the last cut of the day and costs less. That is still the right choice for a saw that lives in a garage or shop. Cordless earns its premium when the work moves: a trim carpenter going room to room, a deck builder in a backyard with one outlet, or anyone who would otherwise run a long extension cord across a job.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is batteries. A cordless miter saw is only as good as the pack on it, and the large packs that make these saws work all day are expensive on their own. If you already own several big M18 or 20V/60V batteries, a cordless slider like the Milwaukee 2734-20 or DeWalt's 60V FLEXVOLT DCS781 is easy to justify. DeWalt says the DCS781 makes up to 675 cuts in 3-1/4-inch MDF base on one FLEXVOLT 9.0Ah pack.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not own a platform, pick one before you pick the saw. Our <a href="/best-cordless-tool-brand/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best cordless tool brand guide</a> walks through the ecosystems, and our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">Milwaukee vs DeWalt comparison</a> covers the two most common choices for people buying their first big cordless saw. Ryobi sells smaller 7-1/4-inch ONE+ sliding miter saws that suit light trim and hobby work but not framing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cordless saws also tend to be smaller than their corded equivalents. Most cordless sliders use 7-1/4-inch, 10-inch or 12-inch blades and weigh less than corded 12-inch saws, which is part of their appeal. In return, their vertical capacity is usually lower. Check the base and crown numbers before assuming a cordless saw will cut the same trim your corded saw does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="accuracy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Accuracy: Detents, Fences and Getting Square Cuts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every miter saw can make an accurate cut, and every one of them can be knocked out of square by a drop off a tailgate. The difference between saws is how easy they are to check and adjust, and how well they hold their settings. Positive detents at common angles, a lock that does not shift the table as you tighten it and a fence that is easy to square all matter more than the motor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check a new saw before you trust it. Cut a wide board at 90 degrees, flip one piece over and push the cut edges together. Any gap is double the error. Do the same with a 45-degree cut on two pieces of trim. Most saws arrive close and need a small adjustment to the detent plate or bevel stop, which the manual covers and takes about fifteen minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Slider rails add a second source of error. Worn or loose rails let the head deflect as you push through the cut, and cheap saws are worse at this. The DeWalt, Bosch, Makita and Milwaukee saws here all have reputations for holding square. Budget sliders with thin rails are where you see cuts that are square at the front of the board and off at the back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Technique matters as much as the saw. Let the blade reach full speed before it touches the wood, cut on the downstroke with a slider pulled toward you first, and let the blade stop before you raise it. Raising a spinning blade through the cut is the most common way to chip trim and spoil an otherwise clean miter that would close perfectly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Blade Matters More Than the Saw
          </h2>

          <p>
            The blade that ships with most miter saws is a general-purpose blade with somewhere between 24 and 60 teeth, and it is fine for framing lumber. For trim and furniture work, the biggest improvement you can make to any saw is a quality finish blade. A 10-inch blade with 60 to 80 teeth, or a 12-inch blade with 80 to 100, leaves glue-ready cuts in hardwood and clean edges on painted trim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the blade to the material. Low tooth counts cut faster and run cooler in thick, wet framing lumber. High tooth counts cut slower but cleaner. Negative or low hook-angle teeth are standard on miter saw blades because they are less aggressive and less likely to pull the saw head toward you. Never fit a ripping blade or a table saw blade with an aggressive hook angle to a miter saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some sliders and chop saws can cut aluminum and plastics with the right non-ferrous blade, which helps with window trim, storm doors and composite decking. Do not try to cut steel with a wood blade or an abrasive disc on a wood miter saw. That is a job for a metal-cutting saw or an <a href="/best-angle-grinder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">angle grinder</a> with the right cutoff wheel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blades dull faster than most people think, especially on treated lumber and composite decking. If cuts start burning, chipping or pulling the saw sideways, the blade is the first suspect. A sharpening service is usually cheaper than a new premium blade. Keep one blade for framing and another for finish work so the finish blade stays sharp for when it matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Safety Habits That Prevent Miter Saw Injuries
          </h2>

          <p>
            Miter saws are among the safer stationary saws, but injuries happen in predictable ways. Most involve a hand drifting into the blade path on a short piece, a small offcut thrown by the blade, or a board that was not held flat and closed on the blade. Every one of those is preventable with habits that cost nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep your hands out of the marked no-hands zone on the table, usually about six inches from the blade. If a piece is too short to hold safely outside that zone, cut it from a longer board instead, or use the saw's clamp. Never reach across the blade to clear an offcut while the blade is spinning. Wait for it to stop, then move the piece.
          </p>

          <p>
            Support the work properly. A board that sags or rocks will pinch the blade, and a board with a bow should be cut with the bow against the fence and table so it cannot close on the blade. Long boards need support at the same height as the saw table. Never cut freehand without the fence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the manual's section on sliders. With most sliding saws, the safe technique is to pull the head toward you, lower it, then push it back through the cut. Pulling the blade toward you through the wood lets it climb the board and jump toward you. It is the single most common mistake new slider owners make, and it is why the manual is worth fifteen minutes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 12-inch slider for 2x4 work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you mostly cut framing lumber, fence pickets and standard trim, a 60 lb slider is a burden, not an upgrade. A light 10-inch chop saw makes the same cuts, stores on a shelf and costs a fraction of the price. Buy capacity for the boards you actually cut every month.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the factory settings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Miter saws are shipped, dropped and bumped. Check 90 degrees and 45 degrees with a test cut before your first real project, and again after transporting the saw. A miter that is off by half a degree opens a visible gap on every corner of a room, and adjusting the detent plate takes about fifteen minutes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pulling a slider through the cut</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On most sliding saws, the safe technique is to pull the head out, lower it, and push it back toward the fence through the cut. Pulling a spinning blade toward you lets it climb the board and lurch out of the cut. Read the manual and practice on scrap before cutting expensive trim.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting without supporting long boards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A long board that hangs off one side of the saw will sag, pinch the blade and pull the cut out of square, or tip off the table when the offcut drops. Use a stand with extension supports, or a bench with supports level with the saw table. Two sawhorses and a board are fine in a pinch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping the stock blade for finish work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The blade that comes with most saws is a general-purpose blade designed for framing lumber. On painted trim and hardwood it chips edges and leaves rough cuts. A quality 60 to 100 tooth finish blade is the cheapest way to make any miter saw cut like a much better one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a 10-inch or 12-inch miter saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy a 12-inch slider if you build decks, cut stair treads, handle posts or install tall baseboard and crown. Buy a 10-inch saw for trim, furniture and general homeowner work. The 10-inch blades are cheaper, deflect less and the saws are lighter. A 10-inch slider reaches 12 inches of crosscut, which is enough for most shelving and trim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a sliding miter saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you regularly cut boards wider than about 6 inches. A 10-inch chop saw handles a 2x6 at 90 degrees, which covers framing, fence pickets, deck joists and most baseboard. A slider cuts 2x12 or 2x14 in one pass. If wide boards are rare, finish them with a circular saw and save the money and weight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dual-bevel miter saw worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it for trim work. A dual-bevel saw tilts both ways, so you can bevel both sides of a corner without flipping long boards end for end. For framing, decks and fences, a single-bevel saw is fine because you rarely cut bevels. For whole-house baseboard and crown, dual bevel saves real time and mistakes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless miter saws as good as corded?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best ones are close. The Milwaukee 2734-20 is a full 10-inch dual-bevel slider rated for up to 400 cuts per charge on a large pack. Corded saws still cost less and never run down. Buy cordless if you already own big batteries and work in places without convenient outlets; otherwise a corded saw is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a miter saw replace a table saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A miter saw crosscuts and miters, cutting across the width of a board. It cannot rip a board along its length or cut down sheets of plywood. Most workshops end up with both, or with a miter saw and a circular saw with a straightedge for ripping. Decide which cuts you make most often before buying either.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What blade should I put on my miter saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For framing, the included general-purpose blade is fine. For trim and furniture, fit a finish blade with 60 to 80 teeth on a 10-inch saw or 80 to 100 teeth on a 12-inch saw. Use blades designed for miter saws, with a low or negative hook angle, and a non-ferrous blade for aluminum.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I check if my miter saw is square?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cut a wide board at 90 degrees, flip one half over and push the cut edges together. Any gap shows double the error. For 45 degrees, cut two pieces of trim and dry-fit the corner against a square. Adjust the detent plate or bevel stop per the manual. Recheck after transporting the saw, since bumps shift settings.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

